From b0056b019533cbe7602587c4d3e2958acaeb78cd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 02:43:04 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayer, dvbridge: libplacebo's knee of the BT.2390 curve
 as a choice

The BT.2390 curve has the knee of ITU-R BT.2390 (KS = 1.5 maxLum - 0.5)
since the change before this one. The knee it had until then,
libplacebo's default (KS = 2 maxLum - 1), stays available by its own
name, off by default:
- "BT.2390, libplacebo's knee", a tone mapping method of the player's
  video settings next to "Reference (BT.2390)", for a display without
  HDR;
- the value "BT.2390, libplacebo's knee" of "Tone mapping curve"
  (videoscreen.hadesdvhdr10curve), for the mappings to an HDR10
  display (Dolby Vision as HDR10, HDR10 adapted to the display).

What it trades: it starts the roll-off much lower, so what the display
could show as mastered comes out darker, and what is above the
display's peak keeps more of its gradation. On a neutral ramp it gives
what is above the display's peak 1.7 times the range (in PQ). On real
pictures (profile 7 FEL, the trim pass off) mapped to SDR at 203
cd/m2, the spread of the intensity of what is above the display's
white is 44 % of the source's instead of 35 % (at 100 cd/m2, 39 %
instead of 28 %); where the display can show the source, the picture
is 3.12 Delta E ITP from it on average instead of 0.82 (at 100 cd/m2,
10.2 instead of 3.6).

Chosen, it draws the bytes of the curve before the report's knee: a
curve of the bridge of its own, DVBRIDGE_HDR10_CURVE_BT2390_LIBPLACEBO,
whose parameters are libplacebo's, untouched. The new method is a
method of a reference display wherever "Reference (BT.2390)" is one
(IsReferenceToneMapMethod()): HDR10, HDR10+, HLG and Dolby Vision go to
SDR through the bridge, and what the bridge does not take Kodi's own
shader draws with Hable. A mapping to SDR is taken again when the
method changes between the two knees while a video plays, as when it
changes to one of Kodi's own methods. Low-Latency keeps the report's
knee: it has no curve to choose.

The log names the curve. The help of "Tone mapping curve" says what
each curve does, and no longer says that a shot brighter than the
display is made darker as a whole: libplacebo does that only where it
is asked to (dynamic_target, libplacebo-9943), and Hades does not ask.
The help of the SDR target names the new method too. New strings: core

Measured on the software bridge (Mesa llvmpipe), with the image's
libplacebo:
- a neutral ramp of every 10-bit code of a plain PQ picture, masters
  of 1000, 4000 and 10000 cd/m2 to SDR whites of 100, 203 and 300 and
  HDR10 displays of 600 and 1400 cd/m2: the new curve gives the bytes
  of the BT.2390 curve before the report's knee in all 15 cases, and
  is within 0.17 of a code of the EETF with KS = 2 maxLum - 1; the
  report's knee and the spline give the same bytes as without this
  change (15 and 15);
- 16 pictures of two films (profile 7 FEL with the enhancement layer)
  to SDR at 203 and 100 cd/m2 and to HDR10 displays of 600, 1000 and
  1387 cd/m2 (the Low-Latency peak that an LG C5 declares), the trim
  pass on: the new curve gives the bytes of before in all 80, and the
  report's knee the bytes of the change before this one in all 80;
- the choice read in the code of the player, the window system and
  the renderer, the setting and the strings (34 checks; six mutants
  of them caught), and the curves of the bridge (17 checks); a mutant
  that gives the new curve the report's knee fails 14 of the 15 ramps.
